<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">AlUla</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Al-Ula</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">AlUla is an oasis town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> Province, north-western Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, on the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> route. Its valley was the seat of the kingdoms of Dedan and Lihyan in the 1st millennium BCE, and nearby lies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hegra">Hegra</a>, the Nabataean city that became Saudi Arabia's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2008. Its old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> town was inhabited until the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alula.jpg" alt="AlUla" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sammy Six · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Ula_(6748577917).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>AlUla lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, 350 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in a valley of vertical sandstone cliffs that contrast with the palm groves of its oasis. It was a market city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade route that linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The cliffs carry rock art: a survey of about 1,000 square kilometres around the valley recorded 19,802 identifiable panels, mostly engravings, spanning more than 12,000 years.</p><p>The earliest images show life-sized wild animals, including wild <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, equids and ibex. Later phases add <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and hunting scenes, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> onward domesticated camels dominate, often with riders, alongside horsemen, battles and, in the latest periods, objects such as firearms and motor vehicles. The Jabal Ikmah inscriptions, nearly 300 texts from the second half of the first millennium BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, Dadanitic, Thamudic and Minaic, were inscribed on the UNESCO Memory of the World register in 2023.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oasis has been settled permanently since 5000 BCE. It stands on the site of the biblical Dedan, and the Dedanite kingdom is dated to the 7th and 6th centuries BCE. The Harran Stela tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, last ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, campaigned in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> in 552 BCE or somewhat later and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a>, Dedan and Yathrib. The kingdom of Lihyan then held the region for about four hundred years, until around 100 BCE. The Nabataeans were lords of the region until 106 CE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> annexed it into its province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea">Arabia Petraea</a>.</p><p>In the Islamic period al-Mabiyat, some 20 kilometres away, became the commercial centre from about 650 CE and declined before 1230. In the 13th century the old walled town of al-Dirah was built, reusing stones from the Dedanite and Lihyanite ruins; at its largest it held more than 1,000 houses joined to form a defensive wall. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> railway between 1901 and 1908, with stations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hegra">Hegra</a> and al-Ula. The last family is said to have left the old town in 1983.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Antonin Jaussen and Raphaël Savignac, French priests, visited three times in 1907, 1908 and 1910 and collected many Lihyanite, Minaean, Thamudic and Nabataean inscriptions; their work became the basis of later study. Charles Doughty described the town in 1876, and Charles Huber and Julius Euting followed.</p><p>Today AlUla is the capital of a governorate of Medina Province and had a population of 60,103 in 2022 for the municipality as a whole. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hegra">Hegra</a>, 22 kilometres to the north, is Saudi Arabia's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The Royal Commission for al-Ula, set up in July 2017, directs conservation and tourism development.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Ula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dedan, the ancient name of the oasis at <b>al-Ula</b>, is mentioned in the Hebrew Bible as a trading people.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dedan">Kingdom of Dedan</a></li><li><blockquote>Lihyan succeeded the Kingdom of Dedan at the oasis of <b>al-Ula</b>, probably in the late 6th or 5th century BCE, after the Babylonian occupation of the Hejaz ended.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lihyan">Kingdom of Lihyan</a></li></ul></section>
